candidates.body:
Another variant of the method-default-reads-`self` problem mentioned in the comments here, which fails earlier — at compile time with an internal error rather than the runtime `interface conversion` panic — when the default expression is a binary expression over `self.n`:

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

class Counter {
    int n = 4;

    function get(int a = self.n * 10) returns int {
        return a;
    }
}

public function main() {
    Counter c = new;
    io:println(c.get());
}
```

Actual (on `main` @ cc62d6d68b9330e342c17d466d915e5064b02528):
```
fatal[INTERNAL_ERROR]: expression type not resolved
 --> repro.bal:6:35
  |
6 |     function get(int a = self.n * 10) returns int {
  |                                   ^^
```

Expected: either a proper diagnostic (spec §6.8.1.2 binds `self` within a method-defn-body; a defaultable parameter's default expression can only refer to earlier parameters, so `undefined symbol 'self'` would be appropriate), or, if `self` is meant to be in scope there, output `40`. An internal error is wrong either way. The plain `int a = self.n` form still reaches runtime and panics with `interface conversion: values.BalValue is nil, not *values.List`.
